etin includes more comprehensive data, covering the fiscal years from 1938 to 1947. The public works expenditures show a clear trend of decline starting from 1940, with a significant drop in 1945, followed by a slight increase in 1946. The data reflects the impact of World War II on public works spending, with a notable reduction in expenditures during the war years.\n\nI'm focusing on the absolute difference in public works spending between 1934 and 1946. The 1947 bulletin provides the revised figures, accounting for key programs like PWA and housing, while excluding wartime spending. I'll need to carefully locate the specific 1934 figure in the document, comparing the 1942 and 1947 documents to ensure accuracy.\n\nThe absolute difference between 407 and 549 million dollars is 142 million dollars, which becomes more pronounced when considering the potential exclusion of wartime spending.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_a684aad9f94cca05","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"1934\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1947_08_page_24.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
